# Every persisted identifier column an entity declares, classified by whether it holds the identifier of a
# NATURAL PERSON — and, when it does, naming the file obliged to erase it.
#
# The key is `<Fqcn>::$<property>`, the granularity of the fact itself. A coarser key (the entity, the table)
# averages over columns with different meanings and hands a green light to whatever the average hides.
#
# The rule this file exists to enforce: a persisted reference to a person must have an identified owner of its
# erasure, and that owner must actually execute it. Cross-context references are by id and never by mapped
# association, so nothing physical drags a person's id out of a foreign table when their identity is deleted —
# `membership.user_id` and `iam_invitation.invited_user_id` deliberately carry no foreign key. That makes the
# obligation distributed, and a distributed obligation is only safe when the moment it arises is impossible to
# pass in silence. Every bounded context that comes to touch a person will mint another one of these columns.
#
# Format, one per line:
#   <Fqcn>::$<property> => non-person
#   <Fqcn>::$<property> => person :: <path, relative to api/, of the file that erases it>
#
# There is deliberately no `person` without an owner. An unowned person reference IS the violation this file
# exists to catch, so it cannot also be a spelling that passes. If a sanctioned exception is ever needed it
# gets a verb of its own, never a silent `person`.
#
# The gate (PersonReferenceGateTest, run by `make php.lint.person-reference`) fails the build when a
# `Types::GUID` column reaches an entity without a line here, when a line no longer matches any column, when a
# `person` line names a file that does not erase that reference, when a `#[PersonSubjectReference]` declared
# at a property disagrees with the line here, and when a `person` line carries no declaration at its property
# at all.
#
# That last direction has ONE exemption, and it is that one KEY rather than a shape: `User::$id`, and only
# while it is still a primary key. The attribute is named for a REFERENCE — an id of a person held by a row
# that is not them, which no foreign key removes, which is the distributed obligation this file exists to
# make impossible to pass in silence. `identity_user`'s own key carries no such obligation: erasing it IS
# deleting its own row, and the wiring check already proves that happens.
#
# Reading "carries #[ORM\Id]" alone would NOT say that, and the gap is not theoretical: a join table with a
# composite `(userId, organizationId)` key, or a one-to-one extension table keyed by the subject, holds a
# person's id in a row that is not the person. Its orphan is exactly the residue the detective control below
# exists to find, and under the mechanical reading it would need neither the attribute nor a source while the
# build stayed green — one exemption switching off two rules at once. So the rule names the key, and keeps
# `#[ORM\Id]` as a second conjunct: if the person aggregate ever stops being keyed by that property, the
# exemption lifts and the build says so. Exempting by "the property is inherited" instead would key the rule
# to an implementation accident and let any person id dropped into a shared trait escape it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What it deliberately does NOT do. Read this list as the specification of the gate's blind spots — a green
# build proves what is below the line, and nothing above it.
#
#   - IT CANNOT JUDGE THE CLASSIFICATION, AND THE TWO DIRECTIONS OF ERROR ARE NOT SYMMETRIC. Calling a
#     non-person a `person` refutes itself: the line then has to name a file that erases it, and for
#     `organizationId` or `bankId` no such file exists, so the build stops. The opposite direction — writing
#     `non-person` over a column that does hold a person's id — PASSES, because the gate only checks that a
#     classification exists and is wired, never what it means. That is the division of labour the axis is
#     built on: the human classifies, the automation verifies. Architecture review is the control on the
#     half the automation cannot reach.
#   - It does not reach references born in CONFIGURATION. A person id that arrives through a route default, a
#     service argument or a YAML parameter is declared by no property, so no reflection over properties sees
#     it. Closing that is FR9 of the GDPR-hardening epic, deferred with the ownership question it opens.
#   - It does not reach TABLES WITH NO DOCTRINE ENTITY. `audit_log.actor_id`, `audit_log.resource_id`,
#     `audit_log.metadata` and `event_store.aggregate_id` are injected by `postGenerateSchema` listeners and
#     written through raw SQL: no domain property declares them, so this sweep is structurally blind to all
#     four. Only the resource axis has a control of its own (`.audit-resource-types`);
#     `event_store.aggregate_id` has NONE and is a standing leak with its own story. `audit_log.metadata`
#     holds no person id. Ten paths write a row — nine `AuditLogger->log()` call sites plus the
#     change-data-capture listener — of which seven pass a non-empty `metadata`, and exactly one of those
#     ever held a person id: the compliance entry of the identity erasure, which now names its subject as
#     the entry's RESOURCE instead, where the anonymiser that same erasure already runs rewrites it.
#     FOUR THINGS HOLD THAT, AND NONE OF THEM IS A GATE OVER KEY NAMES — a pseudonym is a UUID
#     indistinguishable by shape from a real id, so a shape rule would break the `anonymized_actor_id`
#     contract, and a key-name rule would verify its own declaration:
#       (a) an acceptance assertion that the erased subject's id appears in no row's `metadata`, matched
#           case-insensitively because `metadata` is jsonb TEXT and Postgres normalises no case there;
#       (b) beside it, one that joins `audit_log` against `identity_user`, so an id belonging to somebody
#           still alive is caught too — the assertion in (a) names one subject and cannot see that;
#       (c) the containment rule in PersonReferenceGateTest: no `person` column may sit on an aggregate
#           that opts into change-data-capture, which is the one write path whose content is a property of
#           the entity model rather than of a reviewed call site.
#       (d) PrivacyContext's `@AfterScenario` hook, which makes the same join as (b) after EVERY scenario
#           of the suite rather than inside one of them, so it covers write paths no privacy feature was
#           written for and survives the deletion of the scenario that (a) and (b) live in. Read its exit
#           code, not the tally: a failure in an `AfterScenario` hook marks no step and no scenario.
#     Both acceptance halves live in one scenario of `api/features/backoffice/users/erase.feature`, and
#     that scenario is deletable in a one-line diff with every gate still green — (c) and (d) are the two
#     that outlive it, (c) mechanically and (d) by being attached to the suite rather than to a feature.
#     The trigger to revisit is ANY write path that puts a person id
#     in `metadata` again; the count that decides whether a declarative key registry earns its cost is how
#     many exist at once, which today is zero.
#   - It only sees `Types::GUID` columns and TO-ONE ASSOCIATIONS (`#[ORM\ManyToOne]`, `#[ORM\OneToOne]` —
#     read at the association attribute, because `#[ORM\JoinColumn]` is optional and the plainer form is the
#     one written by default). A person's id persisted in a plain string column, or in a column whose type
#     Doctrine infers rather than the property declaring it, is invisible here — a rename of the type is
#     enough to leave the sweep, and nothing below this line would change.
#   - It does not reach a column that belongs to ANOTHER table than the declaring entity's: a `#[ORM\JoinTable]`
#     behind a `#[ORM\ManyToMany]` holds both ids in a table no property declares, and an `#[ORM\Embeddable]`
#     carries its columns into whatever embeds it while declaring no `#[ORM\Entity]` of its own, so neither
#     side of that pair enters the universe.
#   - It derives from ONE class per file, named after its path. Doctrine discovers entities by including the
#     file and reading every class declared in it, so a second entity in the same file, or a class whose name
#     does not match its filename, is mapped by Doctrine and unseen here.
#   - The wiring check proves a CALL EXISTS, not that it RUNS, and NOT THAT IT IS SCOPED TO THE SUBJECT. It
#     reads the declared owner's comment-stripped source for a collaborator naming the entity and a deletion
#     called on it; a call behind a condition that is never true satisfies it, and so does one that deletes by
#     some other predicate entirely — `deleteExpired($now)` on the right repository passes. Runtime
#     reachability and correct scoping are proven by the owning context's acceptance scenario, not here.
#   - The wiring check reads a COLLABORATOR NAMING THE ENTITY, so it resolves at entity granularity while the
#     key is per property: it cannot tell `Membership::$userId` from `Membership::$organizationId`, and would
#     accept the same owner for both. Harmless while an owner deletes the whole row; it stops being harmless
#     the first time a person column is scrubbed in place instead. For the same reason it cannot recognise an
#     owner that erases through raw DBAL, because a `Connection` names no entity.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DETECTIVE CONTROL, and what IT does not see. Every `person ::` line above EXCEPT THE SUBJECT'S OWN
# PRIMARY KEY is also enumerated against the reconciler that reads the rows back
# (`identity:gdpr:reconcile-subject-references`): each such column must have a
# `Shared\Privacy\Application\PersonReferenceSource` declaring its key, and no source may declare a key this
# file does not carry. That comparison is the only thing separating "the erasure is WRITTEN" — which is all
# the checks above prove — from "the row is GONE". Its own limits:
#
#   - IT INHERITS EVERY BLIND SPOT ABOVE, because it is enumerated against THIS FILE. A person id in a column
#     no entity declares, or born in configuration, is missing from the registry and therefore missing from
#     the control, in exactly the same silence. Concretely, and worth naming because the exception below
#     invites the opposite reading: `audit_log.actor_id` and `event_store.aggregate_id` hold person ids,
#     have no entity, and are OUTSIDE the control. It reaches four columns plus one.
#   - IT PROVES THE WIRING IS DECLARED, NOT THAT THE COLLECTION WAS BUILT. The coverage gate compares declared
#     keys against these lines and reads `config/services.yaml` for the tag and the tagged iterator — all
#     static. Whether the compiled container actually holds one source per line is pinned by
#     `tests/Functional/Shared/Privacy/PersonReferenceCollectionFunctionalTest.php`, which runs under
#     `make php.unit` and NOT under `make php.lint.person-reference`. A green gate alone leaves an `exclude:`
#     in the `Erpify\` service resource undetected.
#   - IT PROVES A SOURCE EXISTS, NEVER THAT IT READS THE RIGHT COLUMN. A source whose SQL pointed at another
#     table would satisfy every check here and report an empty axis for ever. That the query matches the
#     column is proven by each source's functional test against real Postgres.
#   - THE READS ARE NOT ONE SNAPSHOT. Each source queries its own table, then one probe resolves which of the
#     collected ids are live; an erasure committing inside that window is reported as a divergence it is not.
#     It self-corrects on the next run and the repair is idempotent, so the cost is a transient false
#     positive — but a finding is not proof that the state was ever wrong.
#   - IT DETECTS ERASURE RESIDUE, NOT ILLEGITIMATE REFERENCES. A finding is an id that resolves to no live
#     identity. A row that names a person who still exists but should no longer be referenced is a correct
#     row to this control, whatever the business rule says.
#   - IT CANNOT TELL AN ERASURE THAT NEVER RAN FROM ONE A LATER WRITE UNDID. Both leave the same dangling id,
#     and nothing here dates it or attributes it, so a finding says WHAT is wrong and never HOW it got there.
#   - IT ONLY READS. Repair is a deliberate operator act through the erasure use case; a green run therefore
#     means somebody repaired, never that the write path which created the residue was fixed.
#   - THE SCHEDULED ARM ALARMS WITH COUNTS AND NEVER IDS. `identity_maintenance` runs it daily and the
#     divergence line carries the total, the axes checked and the count per axis — the ids stay in the CLI,
#     because a control enforcing that no person's id outlives its erasure cannot mint copies in log storage,
#     which has its own retention and no declared owner of its erasure. So the alarm says GO LOOK; the CLI is
#     where an operator learns which subjects, and its output is ephemeral.
#   - A FAILED READ IS NOT A CLEAN RUN, and the two arms keep them apart differently: the handler lets
#     `PersonReferenceProbeFailed` propagate (Messenger logs `critical` and Sentry captures it — a log line
#     would not reach Sentry), the command exits `INVALID` instead of `FAILURE`. Nothing about any subject is
#     established on that path, so it is never grounds to repair.
#   - NOTHING PROVES THE SCHEDULE IS ALIVE. `make php.lint.schedule-consumption` asserts the transport name is
#     in both compose consume commands; it cannot see whether the worker runs or whether a tick was ever
#     delivered. A control nobody invokes still detects nothing, and that half stays operational.
#   - `audit_log.resource_id` IS IN THE CONTROL BUT NOT IN THIS FILE, and that carve-out has EXACTLY ONE SLOT.
#     It has no entity, so it can never be a key here; the reconciler holds it as a hardcoded collaborator
#     instead, and its presence is pinned by the reconciler's own tests rather than by the coverage gate.
#     A second no-entity axis cannot simply be added as a source — the gate would reject a key this file does
#     not carry — so it needs the registry to grow a verb for no-entity columns first. That is a conscious
#     decision, not a line to add in passing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Surrogate identifiers of aggregates that are not people. `BankAccount::$id` is the one worth arguing: the
# account is the codebase's only entity carrying `#[PersonalData]` columns, and it has an erasure use case of
# its own. It stays non-person because the id denotes the ACCOUNT — what is personal there is the holder's
# name and IBAN, which is the treatment axis (`#[PersonalData]` + crypto-shredding), not this one.
Erpify\Backoffice\Bank\Domain\Entity\Bank::$id => non-person
Erpify\Backoffice\BankAccount\Domain\Entity\BankAccount::$id => non-person
Erpify\Organization\Organization\Domain\Entity\Organization::$id => non-person
Erpify\Organization\Membership\Domain\Entity\Membership::$id => non-person
Erpify\Iam\Invitation\Domain\Entity\Invitation::$id => non-person
Erpify\Iam\Session\Domain\Entity\Session::$id => non-person
Erpify\Iam\Identity\Domain\Entity\PasswordResetToken::$id => non-person

# References between aggregates that are not people.
Erpify\Backoffice\BankAccount\Domain\Entity\BankAccount::$bankId => non-person
Erpify\Organization\Membership\Domain\Entity\Membership::$organizationId => non-person
Erpify\Iam\Invitation\Domain\Entity\Invitation::$organizationId => non-person
Erpify\Iam\Session\Domain\Entity\Session::$organizationId => non-person

# The person's own identifier. `identity_user.id` IS the subject, so classifying it non-person would be the
# most consequential false statement this file could carry. Its owner hard-deletes the row: the whole
# aggregate is the personal datum, so there is nothing left to scrub afterwards. It carries no
# `#[PersonSubjectReference]` because the property is declared in the shared `Identifiable` trait, and
# annotating it there would propagate the declaration to all eight entities that use it.
Erpify\Iam\Identity\Domain\Entity\User::$id => person :: src/Iam/Identity/Application/EraseIdentitySubject.php

# References to a person held by another aggregate. Each row is deleted outright rather than scrubbed:
# neither table has a business meaning that survives the person it is about.
Erpify\Iam\Identity\Domain\Entity\PasswordResetToken::$userId => person :: src/Iam/Identity/Application/EraseIdentitySubject.php
Erpify\Iam\Session\Domain\Entity\Session::$userId => person :: src/Iam/Session/Application/PurgeUserSessions.php

# The two that cross a bounded context. NO column anywhere in the schema references `identity_user` — the
# database holds exactly two foreign keys, `bank_account.bank_id` and `membership.organization_id` — so
# deleting an identity cascades to nothing at all, and every line above owes its erasure to a use case rather
# than to a constraint. What distinguishes these two is not the absence of a key but that, until now, they
# had no owner. Each is erased by its owning context's published use case, chained into the erasure
# transaction — the person's context orchestrates, but never reaches into another's storage.
Erpify\Organization\Membership\Domain\Entity\Membership::$userId => person :: src/Organization/Membership/Application/PurgeUserMembership.php
Erpify\Iam\Invitation\Domain\Entity\Invitation::$invitedUserId => person :: src/Iam/Invitation/Application/PurgeUserInvitations.php
